## Lesson 4: the same grid, four questions {#sec-st-red}

| Question | Combine | Arrows |
|---|---|---|
| Longest common subsequence | max, diagonal $+ 1$ on a match | all three |
| Minimum delete / insert cost | min, pay the letter you drop | all three; a match is free |
| Edit distance | min, pay 1 unless the letters match | all three |
| Count of embeddings of $t$ in $s$ | $+$ | skip $s$, or match |
| Can $s_3$ be coloured as $s_1$ then $s_2$? | OR | the letter comes from one string |

Delete-only distance (no replacements) is $n + m - 2 \cdot \mathrm{LCS}$: every unmatched letter of either string is deleted, and the matched letters are the LCS. A heaviest common subsequence is the LCS recurrence with the letter's weight on the diagonal instead of $+ 1$. Minimum ASCII delete sum is the total of both strings minus twice that weight, because a kept letter is saved on both sides.

## Lesson 5: the pattern becomes a state {#sec-st-auto}

A wildcard and a regular expression are still a grid. `?` is a diagonal that accepts any letter. `*` is a column that means "zero characters" (move left) or "one more character" (move up, stay on the star). `x*` is one token, not a character followed by a star: zero copies jumps two columns left, and one more copy of `x` moves up.

"The string must contain this block" or "must not contain this word" does not fit in $(i, j)$. The text is being built, or scanned, one letter at a time, and the summary of the pattern is how much of it is matched so far. That summary is the state of the Knuth–Morris–Pratt automaton: after every letter, the state is the longest prefix of the pattern that is a suffix of what has been read. The failure links are the borders of the pattern, computed once. Reaching the end of the pattern means the block has occurred. An absorbing copy of that state counts strings that contain it, once, however many times it occurs. Deleting the arrows into that state counts strings that avoid it.

The prefix function of a pattern $p$:

::: {#9d490951 .cell execution_count=6}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def prefix_function(p):
    pi = [0] * len(p)
    k = 0
    for i in range(1, len(p)):
        while k and p[i] != p[k]:
            k = pi[k - 1]
        if p[i] == p[k]:
            k += 1
        pi[i] = k
    return pi

assert prefix_function("(()") == [0, 1, 0]
assert prefix_function("abab") == [0, 0, 1, 2]
assert prefix_function("aabaaab") == [0, 1, 0, 1, 2, 2, 3]
```
:::


$\pi[6] = 3$ on `aabaaab` because `aab` is both a prefix and the suffix (@fig-st-pi). The automaton of `abab` is that function turned into a table: from the matched length, read one letter and land on the next length (@fig-st-auto). State $4$ means the word just occurred. Reading `a` there returns to state $3$, so `abababab` completes the word three times, including the overlaps.

