##### Python

::: {#st-lcs-row .cell execution_count=16}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def lcs_row(a, b):
    if len(a) < len(b):
        a, b = b, a
    prev = [0] * (len(b) + 1)
    for ca in a:
        cur = [0]
        for j, cb in enumerate(b, 1):
            if ca == cb:
                cur.append(prev[j - 1] + 1)
            else:
                cur.append(max(prev[j], cur[j - 1]))
        prev = cur
    return prev[-1]

assert lcs_rec("abcde", "ace") == lcs_memo("abcde", "ace") == 3
assert lcs_tab("abcde", "ace") == (3, "ace")
assert lcs_row("abcde", "ace") == 3
assert lcs_row("abc", "abc") == 3 and lcs_row("abc", "def") == 0
assert lcs_tab("zab", "abz")[0] == 2
```
:::


##### C++

```cpp
int lcsRow(string a, string b) {
    if (a.size() < b.size()) swap(a, b);
    vector<int> prev(b.size() + 1), cur(b.size() + 1);
    for (char ca : a) {
        cur[0] = 0;
        for (int j = 1; j <= (int)b.size(); ++j)
            cur[j] = ca == b[j - 1] ? prev[j - 1] + 1 : max(prev[j], cur[j - 1]);
        swap(prev, cur);
    }
    return prev.back();
}
```
:::

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
**Recursion** follows every way to drop letters. **Memoisation** and the **table** are $O(nm)$ time and memory. **One row** is $O(nm)$ time and $O(\min(n, m))$ memory. $n, m \le 1000$ allows the table, which is what reconstruction needs.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- No letter in common: the corner stays 0, and the walk prints nothing.
- One string is a subsequence of the other: the answer is the shorter length.
- Two equal strings: the answer is the length, and the walk prints the string.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- Returning a cell other than the corner. Every prefix pair has its own LCS. The strings in the statement are the full prefixes.
- A greedy earliest match. `zab` against `abz` scores 1 that way and 2 if both `z`s are skipped.
- Reading the diagonal after the cell above has already overwritten it, in a one-array roll that is not saved. Keep the previous row, or save `prev[j - 1]` before you write.
:::

**Variants.** A common subarray cannot skip, so a mismatch resets the length to 0 instead of taking a max of the drops: [LC 718 · Maximum Length of Repeated Subarray](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-length-of-repeated-subarray/){difficulty="Medium"}. The dot product of two subsequences puts the product on the diagonal and still allows either drop: [LC 1458 · Max Dot Product of Two Subsequences](https://leetcode.com/problems/max-dot-product-of-two-subsequences/){difficulty="Hard"}. Deleting until the two strings are equal, with no replacements, is $n + m - 2 \cdot \mathrm{LCS}$: [LC 583 · Delete Operation for Two Strings](https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-operation-for-two-strings/){difficulty="Medium"}.

::: {.practice}
- [LC 718 · Maximum Length of Repeated Subarray](https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-length-of-repeated-subarray/){difficulty="Medium"}
- [LC 1458 · Max Dot Product of Two Subsequences](https://leetcode.com/problems/max-dot-product-of-two-subsequences/){difficulty="Hard"}
- [LC 583 · Delete Operation for Two Strings](https://leetcode.com/problems/delete-operation-for-two-strings/){difficulty="Medium"}
:::


[**From counting letters to paying for them.** LCS gives every kept letter a weight of 1 and every dropped letter 0. Minimum ASCII Delete Sum charges each dropped letter its code, so the border is no longer 0, and the same grid minimises a cost instead of maximising a length.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: Minimum ASCII delete sum for two strings {#sec-st-p1-ascii}

::: {.p-statement}
Delete characters from `s1` or `s2` until the two strings are equal. The cost of deleting a character is its ASCII code. Return the minimum cost.

$1 \le n, m \le 1000$. On `sea` and `eat` the answer is 231: delete `s` (115) and `t` (116), and both become `ea`. On `delete` and `leet` the answer is 403, by keeping `let`. Keeping the other LCS `eet` costs more.

Source: [LC 712 · Minimum ASCII Delete Sum for Two Strings](https://leetcode.com/problems/minimum-ascii-delete-sum-for-two-strings/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
Equal last letters can be kept for free, because deleting either of them would cost its code and the strings would still need to match. Different last letters force a deletion: pay the code of one of them and solve the remaining prefixes. The empty prefix against a non-empty one pays every remaining code. A 0 on that border would say the letters disappeared for free.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
- **State.** $D[i][j]$ = minimum delete cost to make $s[0..i)$ equal $t[0..j)$.
- **Transition.** Equal last letters: $D[i - 1][j - 1]$. Otherwise $\min(D[i - 1][j] + s[i - 1],\ D[i][j - 1] + t[j - 1])$, with the letters read as codes.
- **Base.** $D[i][0]$ and $D[0][j]$ are prefix sums of the codes. **Answer.** $D[n][m]$.
- **Weight form.** Let $W$ be the heaviest common subsequence. The same deletions cost $(\mathrm{sum}\ s + \mathrm{sum}\ t) - 2W$, because each kept letter is saved twice.
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

