## Pattern 2: edit distance and alignment {#sec-st-p2}

**From keeping to transforming.** The LCS family only keeps or drops letters. Edit distance adds a third arrow, **replace**, a diagonal that is legal even when the letters differ, at a price of 1. The answer is the cheapest path from corner to corner, and the path itself is the alignment.

**Recognise it.** One string must become the other. A column of the alignment can match, replace, insert, or delete. The price of a column is 0 or 1, and the answer is the cheapest path, or the string written by a cheapest path that shares matched letters.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
$dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1]$ when the last letters are equal, and otherwise

$$
1 + \min(dp[i - 1][j],\ dp[i][j - 1],\ dp[i - 1][j - 1]).
$$

Borders are the lengths. A shortest common supersequence is the LCS table walked backwards, writing a letter on every step and writing a match only once.
