## Pattern 4: wildcards and regular expressions {#sec-st-p4}

**From letters to tokens.** Every arrow so far consumed at most one letter of each string. A pattern token like `*` can match any number of text letters. In the grid that is not a search: it is a horizontal arrow (the star is empty) and a vertical arrow that stays on the star (it eats one more letter).

**Recognise it.** The text is a string. The pattern is a string with tokens. The question is whether the pattern matches the whole text. `?` and a letter are diagonals. A star is a column, or, for `x*`, a token that spans two characters of the pattern.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
Wildcard `*`: $dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 1]$ (the star is empty) or $dp[i - 1][j]$ (the star consumes $s[i - 1]$ and stays). `?` or an equal letter: the diagonal.

Regex `x*`: $dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 2]$ (zero copies) or, when $s[i - 1]$ fits `x`, $dp[i - 1][j]$ (one more copy). The star and its letter are one token.
:::

