##### C++

```cpp
int bracketCount(int n, const string& s) {
    const int MOD = 1000000007;
    int m = s.size();
    vector<int> pi(m);                                 // prefix function of s
    for (int i = 1; i < m; ++i) {
        int k = pi[i - 1];
        while (k && s[i] != s[k]) k = pi[k - 1];
        pi[i] = k + (s[i] == s[k]);
    }
    vector<array<int, 2>> go(m + 1);                   // KMP automaton; state m absorbs
    for (int st = 0; st < m; ++st)
        for (int bit = 0; bit < 2; ++bit) {
            char ch = "()"[bit];
            int k = st;
            while (k && s[k] != ch) k = pi[k - 1];
            go[st][bit] = k + (s[k] == ch);
        }
    go[m] = {m, m};
    vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 1, vector<int>(m + 1));
    dp[0][0] = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; ++i) {
        vector<vector<int>> nxt(n + 1, vector<int>(m + 1));
        for (int b = 0; b <= n; ++b)
            for (int k = 0; k <= m; ++k) if (dp[b][k]) {
                if (b < n) nxt[b + 1][go[k][0]] = (nxt[b + 1][go[k][0]] + dp[b][k]) % MOD;
                if (b) nxt[b - 1][go[k][1]] = (nxt[b - 1][go[k][1]] + dp[b][k]) % MOD;
            }
        dp.swap(nxt);
    }
    return dp[0][m];
}
```
:::

:::

::: {.p-complexity}
The uncached recursion is for $n \le 5$. **Memoisation** and the **layers** are $O(n^2 |s|)$ states with two transitions each. $n \le 100$ and $|s| \le 200$ is about $4 \cdot 10^6$ states. Memory is one or two layers, $O(n |s|)$.
:::

::: {.p-edge}
- `s` itself not regular: it can still occur inside a longer regular sequence. Sample 1's pattern drives the balance negative, so at least two opens precede it.
- `s` longer than $2n$, or impossible inside a regular sequence (`(((` of length 4 when $n = 2$): 0.
- Every regular sequence contains `()` when $n \ge 1$ and `s` is `()`. The answer is the Catalan number.
:::

::: {.callout-warning title="Common mistakes"}
- Summing every automaton state at balance 0. Sequences that never saw `s` are included. Sample 2 becomes 5.
- Summing every balance at state `|s|`. Those strings are not regular.
- Restarting the count at every position where `s` might start. `()()()` is then counted three times for `s = ()`.
:::

**Variants.** Forbidding `s` is the same DP with the arrows into state $|s|$ deleted, and the answer is the sum of states $k < |s|$ at balance 0, which is $C_n$ minus this problem's answer. An LCS that must avoid a third string as a substring puts the same automaton on the LCS diagonal: [CF 346B · Lucky Common Subsequence](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/346/B){rating="2000"}.

::: {.practice}
- [CF 346B · Lucky Common Subsequence](https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/346/B){rating="2000"}
:::


[**From requiring the word to forbidding it, between bounds.** Bracket Substring makes the matched state absorbing and counts strings that reach it. Find All Good Strings drops every transition into it instead, and adds two flags, still equal to the lower bound and still equal to the upper bound, which the same letter updates.]{.flow-bridge}

### Problem: Find all good strings {#sec-st-p5-good}

::: {.p-statement}
`s1` and `s2` have length `n`, and `s1` is at most `s2` in dictionary order. Return the number of strings `w` of length `n` with `s1` $\le$ `w` $\le$ `s2` that do not contain `evil` as a substring, modulo $10^9 + 7$.

$1 \le n \le 500$ and $1 \le |\mathrm{evil}| \le 50$. For $n = 2$, `aa`, `da`, `evil = b` the answer is 51. For `leetcode` through `leetgoes` with `evil = leet` the answer is 0. For `gx` through `gz` with `evil = x` the answer is 2: `gy` and `gz`.

Source: [LC 1397 · Find All Good Strings](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-all-good-strings/){.solved difficulty="Hard"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
"Between the two bounds" depends only on whether the prefix written so far is still equal to `s1` and still equal to `s2`. Once a letter is strictly above `s1`, the lower flag drops and later letters are free on that side. "Does not contain `evil`" is the avoid-mode automaton: a transition into the matched state is discarded. The two summaries are updated by the same letter.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
- **Automaton.** `go[k][c]` for `c` in `a..z`. Entering state $|\mathrm{evil}|$ is forbidden.
- **State.** $f(i, k, t_\ell, t_h)$ = ways to fill positions $i..n$ without completing `evil`, from automaton state $k$, still tight on the lower bound, still tight on the upper bound.
- **Transition.** The letter at a tight side is clamped to that bound. A free side runs from `a` to `z`. A letter that would complete `evil` is skipped.
- **Answer.** $f(0, 0, 1, 1)$.
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

