**2. [H]** [LC 1458 · Max Dot Product of Two Subsequences](https://leetcode.com/problems/max-dot-product-of-two-subsequences/){difficulty="Hard"}. Non-empty subsequences. ($[2, 1, -2, 5]$, $[3, 0, -6] \to 18$; $[3, -2]$, $[2, -6, 7] \to 21$; $[-1, -1]$, $[1, 1] \to -1$.)

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
The diagonal adds the product to a previous subsequence, or starts a new one at that product. Drops of either side stay available. The empty product 0 must not win when every product is negative, so the answer is the maximum cell and the cells start from the products themselves.

::: {#80f0d2c1 .cell execution_count=92}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def max_dot(a, b):
    n, m = len(a), len(b)
    dp = [[-(10**18)] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]
    ans = -(10**18)
    for i in range(1, n + 1):
        for j in range(1, m + 1):
            prod = a[i - 1] * b[j - 1]
            dp[i][j] = max(prod, dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1], dp[i - 1][j - 1] + prod)
            ans = max(ans, dp[i][j])
    return ans

assert max_dot([2, 1, -2, 5], [3, 0, -6]) == 18
assert max_dot([3, -2], [2, -6, 7]) == 21
assert max_dot([-1, -1], [1, 1]) == -1
```
:::


