**3. [M]** Longest common subsequence of three strings. (`abcbdab`, `bdcaba`, `badacb` $\to$ 4, for example `bdab`.) Taking an LCS of the first two and then matching the third can miss it: an LCS of the first pair may be the wrong one for the third string.

::: {.callout-note collapse="true" title="Solution"}
One more index. Equal letters on all three strings take the diagonal. Otherwise drop exactly one string.

::: {#8d64ec1a .cell execution_count=93}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def lcs3(a, b, c):
    dp = [[[0] * (len(c) + 1) for _ in range(len(b) + 1)] for _ in range(len(a) + 1)]
    for i in range(1, len(a) + 1):
        for j in range(1, len(b) + 1):
            for k in range(1, len(c) + 1):
                if a[i - 1] == b[j - 1] == c[k - 1]:
                    dp[i][j][k] = dp[i - 1][j - 1][k - 1] + 1
                else:
                    dp[i][j][k] = max(dp[i - 1][j][k], dp[i][j - 1][k], dp[i][j][k - 1])
    return dp[-1][-1][-1]

assert lcs3("abcbdab", "bdcaba", "badacb") == 4
```
:::


